decisions:
  - id: d1
    decision: Bug 1 fixed via parser strip AND defensive aggregator double-strip (belt-and-suspenders)
    rationale: Either fix alone would work; together they guarantee no code path produces ^^ even if future aggregator refactors move the symbol construction. Cheap defense for a class of bug that auto-heals downstream symbols on every reindex.
  - id: d2
    decision: writeAndConfirm truncated HMAC-SHA256 (12 hex) with per-install secret key, not raw content hash
    rationale: Security audit flagged pre-image attack on small YAML files. HMAC with per-install key eliminates the attack; truncation to 12 chars keeps envelopes compact. hashHint is documented advisory, not cryptographic — it answers "did the write land identically" not "prove integrity".
  - id: d3
    decision: Consistency manifest transformation classes only, never gate/route content
    rationale: Security audit required this guardrail. Integration test feeds crafted secret gate names and asserts manifest JSON doesn't contain them. ConsistencyTracker has a basename sanitizer as belt-and-suspenders. Safe for telemetry pipeline.
  - id: d4
    decision: PARADIGM_STRICT default OFF in v5.38.0, flip ON in v5.39.0 based on field observation
    rationale: Strict mode turns previously-lenient transformations (duplicate keys, Array coercion) into hard errors. Enabling by default in a minor would break existing projects without warning. One release of field observation is the safe path; v5.39.0 flip with deprecation notice in v5.38.x CHANGELOG.
  - id: d5
    decision: writeAndConfirm applied to only 5 handlers in v5.38.0, remaining 7 purpose handlers deferred
    rationale: 5-handler scope matches architect's plan and keeps the release testable. Remaining handlers (add_aspect, add_signal, add_flow, add_gate, add_state, rename, init) use v5.37.11 inline verification which is sound but not envelope-uniform. Uniformity ships in v5.38.x or v6.0.
  - id: d6
    decision: classifyYamlError lives in portal-core, both paradigm-mcp and paradigm CLI import from there
    rationale: Resolves v5.37.12 Finding 4 drift risk. Single source of truth eliminates the risk that MCP and CLI classify a given YAMLException differently — which would have confused the stop hook vs. paradigm portal check disagreement scenarios.
  - id: d7
    decision: Documentor + builder ran in parallel; Claude manually applied 2 TS-string surface polish fixes after documentor handoff
    rationale: Documentor's scope is docs-only; two string-in-TS surfaces (MCP guidance.ts + HowItWorksSection.tsx) were flagged as high-impact (every MCP agent reads guidance.ts; landing page is public) but technically TypeScript. Inline fix preserved velocity without breaching agent scope contract.
learnings:
  - 'Pattern reflection caught a framing error: Jinx''s v5.37.11 prevention plan was too narrow ("silent no-op") when the actual class is "silent round-trip failure". The right framing is any lossy transformation in the read-write-reindex pipeline where the indexer fails closed. v5.38.0''s consistency manifest makes every lossy transform visible; that''s the architectural fix for the class, not just the instances.'
  - HMAC with per-install key is the right primitive for small-file write verification. Raw hash is pre-image-attackable on tiny YAML files (many gate-name combinations produce the same hash prefix). Truncated HMAC eliminates the attack with minimal envelope bloat. Pattern worth reusing for any write-verify in Paradigm where the file is sensitive config.
  - 'Parallel builder + documentor worked cleanly when the scope boundary was explicit. Documentor stayed out of TS; builder stayed out of markdown. Two TS-string surfaces flagged by documentor (guidance.ts + HowItWorksSection.tsx) needed a third-party (me) to bridge the gap. Rule: when a TS string literal IS content, not structure, flag explicitly at task kickoff so the right agent owns it.'
  - Belt-and-suspenders on Bug 1 (parser strip + aggregator defensive strip) costs almost nothing and guarantees no ^^ symbol survives even if future refactors move symbol construction. Cheap defense for a class of bug that would re-emerge otherwise.
  - PARADIGM_STRICT default OFF in the intro release + ON in the next minor with CHANGELOG deprecation is the disciplined path for fail-fast flags. Prevents surprise breakage while creating a forcing function for the strict-by-default future.
